  "text_md": "Who lukes to the lefte syde, wheune his horse launches, With the lyghtc of the sonne men myghtesee his lyrere.\n\nLAUNDE. A plain place in an unploughed plain park ; a lawn. Saltus, 9 lawnd; Nominale MS\n\nWher the dragoun duelled tho.\n\nGvof Warwike, P. 262.\n\nFor to hunt at the hartes in thas hye laundes In Glamorgane with glee, thare gladchipe was evere. Movte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 53\n\n(2) A washer. to wash. Buandiére, launderer\\_ Hollybands   Dictionarie, 1593 Laundring gold , washing it. Also,\n\nLAUNDER. (1) Any kind of or channel for conveying water. Var: dial. gutter\n\nLAUNDRE A laundress. Palsgrave.\n\nLAUREAT. Crowned with laurel . The laureatship at our universities was a degree in grammar,   including poetry and rhetoric; so called because the person who graduated was presented with a wreath of laurel.\n\nLAUP. To Yorksh. leap.\n\nLAUREOLE \\_ Spurge-laurel (A-n:)\n\nLAURER. A laurel. Chaucer\n\nLAUS. Loose. (A-s:)\n\nThat worthy was the lautcr to have Of poetrie, and the palme toatteyne.\n\nLAUTER The laurel. (A.-N)\n\nLydgate, MS. Ashnole 39,f. 48.\n\nMS. Coll. Trin. Cantab. [.67.\n\nCaught ; received. (A.-s) Thenne was Marie Joseph bitauzt, And he hỈr In spousaile lauzt\n\nLAVALTOE. Same as Lavolla, 9 V For lo the liveless Jacks lavaltoes take\n\nBrome ed, 1661, p. 133. Songs,\n\nAt that sweet musick which themsclves do make.\n\nLAVANDRE. laundress tretise for Reliq: Antiq. i.26.\n\nLAVAS:. Lavisb. Romeus and Juliet, P.20.\n\nLAVANT. A landspring: South.\n\nLAVAST. Uninclosed stubble. Kent.\n\n(2) To lade or draw water. Chaucer. Also, to DOur, as in Perceval,  2250; to wash, Piers Ploughman, p. 273.\n\nLAVE. (1) The rest the remainder. North.\n\nTo gutter, as a candle. Wilts.\n\nLÁVE-EARED. or flap-eared See Topsells Beasts, P 366 ; Hawkins, iii. 357 Lavelugged, Northumb. Holloway   has lapeared in use in Sussex and Hants. Longr\n\nTo or down. Hall hang; flap\n\nLAVEER To work ship thc   wind. An old sea term against\n\nLAVENDER